1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,500
Every screenshot you have seen in this section came from a real build, tested before

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:07,217
a word of narration existed.

3
00:00:07,217 --> 00:00:09,914
The trigger's output was not left to chance.

4
00:00:09,914 --> 00:00:14,758
It was pinned: forty emails of ordinary noise, and one real question, buried on

5
00:00:14,758 --> 00:00:16,781
purpose, the way it actually was.

6
00:00:16,781 --> 00:00:20,521
Why test against frozen mail instead of just switching it on?

7
00:00:20,521 --> 00:00:23,832
Because a live inbox is the worst possible test bench.

8
00:00:23,832 --> 00:00:28,982
It sends you whatever it feels like, never the tricky case you need twice, and every

9
00:00:28,982 --> 00:00:31,434
experiment costs a real call and a wait.

10
00:00:31,434 --> 00:00:36,462
A pinned inbox is the same forty one emails every single run, which means when you

11
00:00:36,462 --> 00:00:41,489
change one word of the system prompt, any change in the outcome was caused by your

12
00:00:41,489 --> 00:00:42,838
word and nothing else.

13
00:00:42,838 --> 00:00:47,866
That is the whole scientific method, borrowed for a Tuesday: hold the world still,

14
00:00:47,866 --> 00:00:49,276
change one thing, look.

15
00:00:49,276 --> 00:00:54,119
You will fix more agent problems with this one habit than with anything clever.

16
00:00:54,669 --> 00:00:57,782
You do not need forty one rows to prove a workflow.

17
00:00:57,782 --> 00:01:02,482
You need enough variety to catch a mistake: at least one email that should be

18
00:01:02,482 --> 00:01:04,924
flagged, and several that should not be.

19
00:01:04,924 --> 00:01:09,685
If your test only ever contains obvious urgent emails, you will never find out

20
00:01:09,685 --> 00:01:12,981
whether the agent also flags a newsletter by accident.

21
00:01:12,981 --> 00:01:17,864
Write your test emails yourself, from memory of your own real inbox, rather than

22
00:01:17,864 --> 00:01:22,747
inventing generic filler: the value of the test is exactly how much it resembles

23
00:01:22,747 --> 00:01:23,540
your Tuesday.

24
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:25,493
The borderline one matters most.

25
00:01:25,493 --> 00:01:30,499
Every inbox has a category that fools humans, and if yours is in the test set, you

26
00:01:30,499 --> 00:01:35,565
get to watch how the agent handles it for free, forever, before it ever handles the

27
00:01:35,565 --> 00:01:36,114
real one.

28
00:01:36,664 --> 00:01:41,672
Six emails, and notice that only one of them tests whether the agent flags well.

29
00:01:41,672 --> 00:01:46,742
The other five all test whether it refrains well, and that ratio is correct: in a

30
00:01:46,742 --> 00:01:51,876
real inbox, almost everything is a thing to ignore, so most of the ways your agent

31
00:01:51,876 --> 00:01:54,567
can be wrong are ways of flagging too much.

32
00:01:54,567 --> 00:01:58,386
A test set that mirrors that lopsidedness is testing the job.

33
00:01:58,386 --> 00:02:01,453
One that is all urgent drama is testing the demo.

34
00:02:02,003 --> 00:02:06,939
That right hand column is not a flaw in the method, it is the reason Section 7

35
00:02:06,939 --> 00:02:07,382
exists.

36
00:02:07,382 --> 00:02:10,546
Pinned data proves the machine is built correctly.

37
00:02:10,546 --> 00:02:15,482
It cannot replace watching it run against a real week of real emails, which is

38
00:02:15,482 --> 00:02:17,570
exactly what a trial task is for.

39
00:02:17,570 --> 00:02:22,380
One check in the left column deserves its own minute, because its failure is

40
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:23,013
invisible.

41
00:02:23,013 --> 00:02:28,265
Fields mapping correctly means the email's body actually arrives inside the prompt.

42
00:02:28,265 --> 00:02:33,264
When the mapping is wrong, nothing goes red: the model receives a prompt with a

43
00:02:33,264 --> 00:02:38,327
blank where the body should be, judges the blank, and answers SKIP with complete

44
00:02:38,327 --> 00:02:39,023
confidence.

45
00:02:39,023 --> 00:02:43,895
Every box green, every answer wrong, the exact case Section 2.3 warned about.

46
00:02:43,895 --> 00:02:48,958
The defence takes ten seconds: on any test run, open the agent node and read the

47
00:02:48,958 --> 00:02:54,274
prompt as the model received it, resolved, with the real subject and body sitting in

48
00:02:54,274 --> 00:02:54,463
it.

49
00:02:54,463 --> 00:02:59,399
Seeing the actual words in the actual slots, once, is worth fifty green ticks.

50
00:02:59,949 --> 00:03:03,383
And if your pinned run flags two out of six instead of one?

51
00:03:03,383 --> 00:03:06,060
That is not failure, that is the loop working.

52
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:10,891
Read the agent's stated reason for the extra flag, find the sentence in your system

53
00:03:10,891 --> 00:03:14,441
prompt that let it through, tighten that sentence, run again.

54
00:03:14,441 --> 00:03:19,213
The run is free, the inbox is frozen, so the only thing changing is your briefing,

55
00:03:19,213 --> 00:03:23,054
and most prompts settle after two or three passes of exactly this.

56
00:03:23,054 --> 00:03:27,652
Tuning on pinned data is where you learn the skill you will use for the life of

57
00:03:27,652 --> 00:03:32,483
every agent you ever run: reading a wrong answer for the instruction that permitted

58
00:03:32,483 --> 00:03:32,657
it.

59
00:03:32,657 --> 00:03:37,371
With the wiring proven, the only thing left is the one step this course cannot do

60
00:03:37,371 --> 00:03:41,562
for you: connecting it to a mailbox and a model that are actually yours.

61
00:03:41,562 --> 00:03:45,170
That is next lecture, and it is where this agent becomes real.
